{"study_accession":"PRJNA1109756","secondary_study_accession":"SRP506786","title":"Pre-Treatment peripheral Immune composition can accurately predict CAR-T outcomes in patients with Large B-Cell Lymphoma","description":"Chimeric Antigen Receptor (CAR)-T cell therapy has revolutionized the treatment landscape for relapsed/refractory B-cell malignancies. Despite its success, approximately 60% of patients experience treatment failure, underscoring the need to better understand the determinants of response and resistance. We performed single-cell RNA-sequencing of pre-treatment peripheral blood samples and anti-CD19 CAR-T products from 57 diffuse large B-cell lymphomas (DLBCL), correlating molecular and cellular features with clinical outcomes. At the time of leukapheresis, responders presented elevated levels of CD16+ monocytes and CD4+ effector memory T cells. In contrast, non-responders showed an inflammation-driven gene expression signature across T-cell and myeloid compartments, marked by upregulation of TNF-α response signaling pathways. Notably, the presence of malignant or healthy B cells (13 of 57 patients) was strongly associated with a favorable response. These findings shed light on the immune landscape conducive to successful CAR-T therapy and offer a molecular framework for developing personalized tools to improve patient selection, stratification, and the design of next-generation CAR-T treatments. Overall design: We performed single-cell RNA sequencing of CD45+ sorted PBMC samples collected at the time of leukapheresis from 57 Diffuse Large B Cell patients, and their matching infusion product, receiving anti CD19 CAR-T products axicbtagene ciloleucel (axi-cel), tisagenlecleucel (tisa-cel) , and a Point-of-Care CD28 -costimulation based product, and correlated their molecular and cellular features with treatment outcome. Please note that the Bulk_* samples were only used as a reference genome to demultiplex the cells from the 10x sequencing. Code supporting the analyses performed in this paper is available at https://github.com/AmitLab/CAR_T.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":21,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN47132961","study_accession":"PRJNA1109756","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Bulk_Healthy_6","description":"Bulk_Healthy_6","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM8819918","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-09-21","attributes":[["source_name","Peripheral Blood"],["tissue","Peripheral Blood"],["cell_type","PBMCs"],["patient in_sample","Healthy_6"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR32515954","experiment_accession":"SRX27828197","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":14019057,"base_count":1289753244,"first_public":"2025-09-21","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM8819918: Bulk_Healthy_6 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":true},{"sample_accession":"SAMN47132962","study_accession":"PRJNA1109756","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Bulk_Healthy_8","description":"Bulk_Healthy_8","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM8819917","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2025-09-21","attributes":[["source_name","Peripheral Blood"],["tissue","Peripheral Blood"],["cell_type","PBMCs"],["patient in_sample","Healthy_8"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR32515955","experiment_accession":"SRX27828196","library_layout":"SINGLE","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":10364566,"base_count":953540072,"first_public":"2025-09-21","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM8819917: Bulk_Healthy_8 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":true},{"sample_accession":"SAMN47132963","study_accession":"PRJNA1109756","blood_class":"pbmc","class_from":"sample","title":"Bulk_Healthy_9","description":"Bulk_Healthy_9","